+# Decision: How committed changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. Up to 800 staff browsers must then show that revision. A busy market averages two status changes per minute, with bursts of up to 20 edits per second around opening time. The board must show a committed change within 15 seconds, recover the latest board after a browser has been disconnected for up to 20 minutes, and be available 99.5 percent of published event hours.
+
+The delivery route shapes three components at once: what the change store must keep and publish, what the delivery node owns at runtime, and what the browser must do to stay current and to recover. Choosing it late would mean redesigning all three. The cost of choosing wrongly is borne by the small team that operates the board during weekend markets, and it is paid either in on-call connection work or in a recurring vendor dependency.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+All three options meet every standing goal. None is eliminated by a gate, so the decision turns entirely on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor independence** (major): the board's delivery path does not depend on one provider's pricing, availability, or API. The project permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, so no tenet forces this; it is the stated priority of the project's owner for this decision.
+- **Operating burden** (moderate): the work the team carries to keep delivery running at 800 browsers, including connection capacity, reconnect storms, and incident response during events.
+- **Update latency** (minor): how soon a committed edit appears. The 15-second target is already met by every option, so latency beyond it is useful but not required.
+- **Recurring cost** (moderate): ongoing fees that scale with connections or messages.
+- **Availability exposure** (moderate): how many separately operated components sit on the path to the 99.5 percent target, and who controls them.
+- **Design complexity in the change store and browser** (moderate): how much replay, publication, and catch-up machinery the other nodes must carry.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers request changes since their last seen revision every five seconds. Delivery stays plain request/response and holds no per-browser state between requests. The change store answers cursor queries ("revisions after N"); the browser runs a polling loop and advances its cursor.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong**. Nothing beyond the project's own servers is involved.
+- Operating burden: **strong**. No long-lived connections; ordinary request capacity planning applies.
+- Update latency: **weak**. A change can take several seconds to appear, which still meets the 15-second target.
+- Recurring cost: **partial**. No vendor fees, but 800 browsers produce a steady stream of repeated reads regardless of edit rate.
+- Availability exposure: **strong**. Only the project's own request path is involved.
+- Design complexity: **strong**. Cursor queries serve both normal updates and 20-minute recovery with the same mechanism.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest and most operable route, in exchange for visible lag and wasted reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** Each browser holds a socket to a project-operated delivery service, which pushes committed revisions as they are published. The change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably, so no revision is lost between commit and push. Delivery manages socket sessions and connection capacity. A browser that reconnects presents its last seen revision and is caught up by replay before receiving live pushes.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong**. The delivery path runs on infrastructure the project controls.
+- Operating burden: **weak**. The team owns connection capacity for 800 simultaneous sockets and the behavior of reconnect surges, for example when a network blip drops many browsers at once.
+- Update latency: **strong**. Pushes reach connected browsers well inside the 15-second target.
+- Recurring cost: **strong**. Hosting cost only; no per-connection or per-message vendor fees.
+- Availability exposure: **partial**. The project owns the whole path, which also means it owns every failure on it.
+- Design complexity: **weak**. The store needs replayable revisions and reliable publication; the browser needs reconnect and catch-up logic.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates and full control, paid for with the team's own connection and reconnect operations work.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime service holds the browser connections. Delivery wraps the provider: it publishes each committed revision to the provider, and browsers use the provider's subscription model. The change store reconciles what it committed against what was published, since the provider is a separate system that can miss or reorder a publication.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **weak**. Delivery and the browser subscription model are coupled to one provider, and replacing it later reworks both.
+- Operating burden: **strong**. The provider carries connection capacity.
+- Update latency: **strong**. Low-latency pushes comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Recurring cost: **weak**. Fees recur and scale with usage.
+- Availability exposure: **partial**. A provider outage sits on the path to the availability target and is outside the project's control.
+- Design complexity: **partial**. Publication reconciliation is required, though the browser's connection handling is largely provided.
+
+**Summary.** The quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations, at the price of fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets.
+
+Vendor independence is the major criterion and the hosted service is weak on it, which removes that option. Polling is the strongest option on operability and design complexity, and the analysis would select it if operating burden were the overriding concern. It loses because the project's owner is willing to take on socket operations in order to have fast updates, and polling's lag and constant repeated reads are the cost of avoiding that work. Self-hosted sockets win on the two criteria the owner weights most: fast updates and no provider coupling.
+
+The decision accepts that the team operates connection capacity and handles reconnect surges. That is the explicit trade: operating burden is the criterion this option is weakest on, and it was accepted knowingly.
+
+Committing forecloses a stateless delivery path and obligates three nodes:
+
+- **Change store**: replayable event revisions covering at least the 20-minute recovery window (seven-day retention already exceeds it), and reliable publication of each committed revision.
+- **Delivery**: socket session management and connection capacity sized for 800 simultaneous browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second.
+- **Browser sync**: reconnect and catch-up from the last seen revision, with connectivity failures shown to the user rather than mistaken for an unchanged board.
+
+No project tenet decides this choice; both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted. The decision is taken on the owner's stated priority for this choice. If circumstances change, for instance if operating socket capacity proves heavier than expected or a provider's terms become acceptable, this document names the criteria to re-weigh.
